.TH C-XREFRC 5 \" -*- nroff -*-
.SH NAME
 .c-xrefrc \- the project configuration of c-xrefactory
.SH DESCRIPTION

A '.c-xrefrc' file defines one project. The directory holding it is the
project root, and the project is identified by that directory's path.
C-xrefactory finds the file by searching upward from the file the editor
asks about, stopping at the user's home directory, so a '.c-xrefrc' in
the home directory is not read. The cross references are kept in
memory and saved to '.c-xref/db' in the project root.

The file is meant to be checked in together with the project, so it
should not contain absolute paths. Where a location differs between
machines, refer to it through an environment variable (see VARIABLES).

.I CAUTION:
the meaning of the configuration is being reworked, see ADR-0031 in the
c-xrefactory documentation. This page describes the current behaviour.

.SH CONTENTS

The file contains c-xref options (see
.BR c-xref (1)),
separated by blanks or newlines. A comment starts with two slashes
followed by a blank and runs to the end of the line. Options containing
blanks can be enclosed in double quotes.

.I THE PROJECT'S FILES

The project root is always scanned for source files, recursively. Each
line in the file that is a directory name rather than an option adds
another directory, which is scanned the same way. A relative directory
name is resolved against the directory holding the '.c-xrefrc', so
\&'../lib' names a sibling of the project root.

.TP
.B \-prune path
Leave the directory
.I path
and everything below it out of the scan. The path is relative to the
project root and matched exactly: '-prune build' skips '<root>/build'
but not 'src/build'. Use one '-prune' per directory.

.PP
.I SECTIONS

The file may start with a section header, a line '[name]'. The name is
shown as the project's name; without a header, the name of the root
directory is used. Options before the header apply as well. A
\&'.c-xrefrc' holds one project: further sections are not used.

.I VARIABLES

An option can refer to a variable as ${name} or %name%. The name is
looked up first among the variables defined with
.B \-set name value
and then in the environment of the c-xref process. A leading '~/'
expands to the home directory. A name that is not defined is left as
written, without a warning. The predefined variable __BASE holds the
section name.

The editor also reads variables with '-set': the Emacs extension asks
for "compilefile", "compiledir", "compileproject", "run1" ... "run5",
"runthis" and "run" to find the commands for compiling and running the
project.

.I OTHER OPTIONS

The options usually found in a '.c-xrefrc' are '-I' and '-D', which
mean what they mean to the C compiler, and the ones described in
.BR c-xref (1).
A relative '-I' directory is resolved against the working directory of
the c-xref process, so prefer a variable.

.SH MULTIPLE PRE-PROCESSOR PASSES

C sources are often compiled several times with different macro
settings, and code inside #if and #else is never compiled together. For
cross referencing and refactoring both parts are needed, so c-xref can
make several passes through the sources and collect the references from
all of them. An option '-pass<n>' makes the options that follow it,
up to the next '-pass', apply only in pass <n>. C-xref makes as many
passes as the largest <n> used.

Several passes make every parse slower, including the ones done while
editing.

.SH EXAMPLE

---------------------------------------------------------

.br
[solver]
.br
// scan a sibling directory too
.br
\&../commons
.br
-prune build
.br
-I ${TOOLCHAIN}/include
.br
-pass1
.br
-DTARGET=linux
.br
-pass2
.br
-DTARGET=solaris

---------------------------------------------------------

Placed in /home/marian/solver, this defines a project rooted there. It
scans that directory except 'build', and '../commons' too. Include
files are also looked for in the 'include' directory of whatever
TOOLCHAIN is set to in the environment, and every file is parsed twice,
once for each TARGET.

.SH BUGS

A file in a listed directory outside the root is scanned, but when the
editor asks whether it belongs to the project, the answer is no.

A file in a pruned directory, or anywhere under an '-I' directory, is
answered as belonging to the project.

While a project is open, the files under a '.c-xrefrc' in one of its
subdirectories belong to it as well, although opening one of those files
first would open the subdirectory's project.

